Z||000000||Tonker||08-29-2001||03:16 AM||||Hi guys,<P>My '86 GT, 4 spd. is having trouble finding a constant idle. When I first start her up, she revs between 800-1500 RPMS and you can hear the engine speed up and then show down. After driving for a while it calms down a bit and varies only by about 100-200 rpm when idling.<P>I took it to my local GM dealership and they replaced the ignition wires, but it didn't seem to help. I did a search and found something on a "idle air control valve"[http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/008849.html]. Does that sound like the culprit? I'd like to be able to take it to the shop and say with some certainly what they should check since they don't seem to know. <P>As an aside, would it be difficult to do myself (with no prior knowledge of auto-maintenance) since funds are currently is short supply?||||reg||5
Z||000001||starfighter007@msn.com||08-29-2001||11:34 AM||||I would suggest taking it back to the Gm dealer and hae them check the throttle position sensor. Or go to Ogres cave and find out how to check it ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Joe Torma||08-29-2001||11:40 AM||||I'd say unplug the idle air while its "hunting"(that's what GM calles it I think) and see if it stops...  May be your MAP sensor.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/simplesig.gif"> ||||reg||1
